<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Homo erectus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pithecanthropus erectus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Homo erectus is an extinct species of archaic human of the Pleistocene, spanning nearly 2 million years, and the first human species with a humanlike body plan and gait, the first to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the first to wield fire. It evolved in Africa from a population of H. habilis; the oldest identified specimen is a skull from Drimolen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, 2.04 million years old, and the earliest records outside Africa, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, date to between 1.6 and 1.85 million years ago. It invented the Acheulean tool industry, and its last known occurrence is 108,000 to 117,000 years ago in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/homo-erectus.jpg" alt="Homo erectus" width="330" height="337" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Luna04 · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homo_erectus_tautavelensis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Eugène Dubois described the species in 1893 as Pithecanthropus erectus from a skullcap, a molar and a femur found at Trinil in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>; European scientists at first dismissed them as the remains of a non-human ape. In the 1930s Franz Weidenreich noticed their similarity to remains from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, then called Sinanthropus pekinensis, and in 1950 Ernst Mayr's reclassification placed these and many other fossils in Homo erectus. Louis Leakey's Olduvai Hominin 9, found in 1960, was the first of the oldest fossils found across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. The species is now generally considered African in origin, dispersing across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a>, with later African populations giving rise to the modern human lineage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>It evolved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> from a population of Homo habilis, with which it coexisted for about half a million years. The oldest identified specimen is the 2.04 million-year-old skull DNH 134 from Drimolen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. It dispersed out of Africa soon after: Homo erectus georgicus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> 1.78 to 1.85 million years ago, the Indonesian sites Mojokerto and Sangiran 1.6 to 1.8 million years ago, and Yunxian Man in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> 1.77 million years ago. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">Stone tools</a> from Jordan and China that may be 2.48 and 2.1 million years old could indicate an earlier species that left Africa.</p><p>Homo erectus evolved into later species including Homo heidelbergensis, the usual last common ancestor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neanderthal">Neanderthals</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denisovan">Denisovans</a> and modern humans. The youngest population, Homo erectus soloensis, dates to 108,000 to 117,000 years ago in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and appears to have died out when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">savannah</a> corridors closed and tropical jungle took over.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Adults are reconstructed at 141 to 167 cm in height and about 50 kg in weight, with a pronounced brow ridge, thick bones and brains averaging about 1,000 cc in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, and as low as 546 cc at Dmanisi. Fossils are often associated with the butchered remains of large herbivores, and a trackway at Ileret in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> was made by a probably all-male band of over 20 individuals.</p><p>The species is credited with inventing the Acheulean stone tool industry, featuring the handaxe, at the latest 1.95 million years ago. It is usually credited as the first to wield fire, the earliest claimed fire site being Wonderwerk Cave in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> at 1.7 million years old, but fire does not become common in the archaeological record until 300,000 to 400,000 years ago. An engraved shell from Trinil may be the earliest art, dating to 436,000 to 546,000 years ago.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Homo erectus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
